I had a bit trouble understanding this visually until I realized that it basically moves the virtual bottom if the viewport changed the side it is relative to the virtual bottom. In other words: If the viewport used to be above the virtual bottom and is now below, then the virtual bottom is moved and vice versa.

In order to understand this I expressed the boolean condition in an equivalent "numeric" expression. These two lines are equivalent to:
```cpp
const auto delta1 = _virtualBottom - _viewport.BottomInclusive();
const auto delta2 = _virtualBottom - newViewport.BottomInclusive();
// Notes:
// * `delta2 != 0` is required because _virtualBottom and newViewport.BottomInclusive()
//   are only ever compared via < and >
// * `delta1 == 0` is required because _virtualBottom and _viewport.BottomInclusive()
//    are also implicitly compared for == (due to >= / <=)
// * (a ^ b) < 0 is true if a/b have opposing sign bits
delta2 != 0 && (delta1 == 0 || (delta1 ^ delta2) < 0);
```

It seems it doesn't matter whether we check for `delta2 == 0`, because if it is 0, then `_virtualBottom == srNewViewport.Bottom` and the line below won't change anything. This is why we can also write:
```cpp
const auto delta1 = _virtualBottom - _viewport.BottomInclusive();
const auto delta2 = _virtualBottom - newViewport.BottomInclusive();
delta1 == 0 || (delta1 ^ delta2) < 0;
```

With this expression in place I found it easier to understand that the virtual bottom moves if either:
* the virtual bottom is currently following the viewport along (`delta1 == 0`) 
* the new viewport has changed direction and is now on the other side
  relative to the virtual bottom (`(delta1 ^ delta2) < 0`)

Personally I'd write this:
```cpp
// Check if _virtualBottom is between _viewport.BottomInclusive() and newViewport.BottomInclusive().
// `_virtualBottom == newViewport.BottomInclusive()` is dropped from the range,
// because we don't need to update anything in that case.
if ((_viewport.BottomInclusive() <= _virtualBottom && _virtualBottom < newViewport.BottomInclusive()) ||
    (newViewport.BottomInclusive() < _virtualBottom && _virtualBottom <= _viewport.BottomInclusive()) ||
```

_However_, I don't think you have to change anything at all, because the misunderstanding is fundamentally on my side. I think it might stem from me not understanding what you meant with "intersect". In my mind the bottom of the viewport(s) and the virtual bottom are both horizontal lines that can't intersect. But you were talking about the "change" (a vertical vector) which can.
So yeah... Don't change it (unless someone else says something). 🙂
